# Building a rule

Open **Alerts** in the sidebar and press **New rule**, or pick an existing rule
to change it. The editor is one page with a card for each part of the rule.
Nothing takes effect until you press **Save** (or **Create** for a new rule).

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-editor" />

## Identity

<Screenshot
  id="alert-rule-identity"
  callouts={[
    { anchor: 'enabled', label: 'Enabled' },
    { anchor: 'severity', label: 'Severity' },
    { anchor: 'allow-dnd', label: 'Allow through Do Not Disturb' },
  ]}
/>

- **Name** is what the alert says when it reaches you, so make it something
  you will understand half asleep: "Low overnight", not "Rule 3".
- **Description** is optional. Use it to note why the rule exists.
- **Enabled** turns the rule on or off. A rule that is off is never checked,
  and switching a rule off closes any alert it has open.
- **Severity** says how urgent the alert is. It changes what happens when the
  alert fires:

| Severity | What it does |
|----------|--------------|
| **Critical** | Always gets through Do Not Disturb. Use it for the alerts that must never be silenced, such as an urgent low. |
| **Warning** | The default. Silenced by Do Not Disturb unless you tick the box below. |
| **Info** | Sent once and then marked as acknowledged straight away. It will not keep nagging. Use it for reminders, such as an old pump site. |

- **Allow through Do Not Disturb** lets a Warning or Info rule reach you even
  while Do Not Disturb is on. Critical rules always get through, so the box
  makes no difference to them.

## Condition

The condition is what the rule watches for. It reads as a sentence: "Notify
when **all of** / **any of** these are true", followed by one row per
condition.

<Screenshot
  id="alert-rule-sustained-low"
  callouts={[
    { anchor: 'operator', label: 'all of / any of' },
    { anchor: 'sustained', label: 'for at least' },
  ]}
/>

Each row starts with a word saying how it joins the rows above it: **IF** for
the first row, then **AND** or **OR** depending on the choice at the top. Then
comes the thing being checked, with its settings.

Press the add button under the rows to add a condition. Conditions are grouped
by what they watch: glucose, insulin, carbs, your devices, what your pump or
loop is doing, time, and other alerts.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-add-picker" />

Every row has a menu (the **...** button at its end) with the ways you can
change it:

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-row-actions" />

- **Wrap in AND group** and **Wrap in OR group** put the row inside a group of
  its own, so you can build rules like "A, or both B and C". See
  [Combining conditions](/docs/alerts/combining).
- **Wrap in NOT** turns the row into its opposite.
- **Make sustained** adds a "for at least N min" box: the condition then has
  to stay true for that long before it counts. See
  [Conditions that must last](/docs/alerts/sustained).
- **Remove** deletes the row. Holding Shift while hovering a row also shows a
  delete button.

You can drag rows by the handle at their left to reorder them, or drop one onto
a group to move it inside. The order of rows does not change what a rule does.

Every condition, with what it means and when to use it, is on the
[Conditions](/docs/alerts/conditions) page.

<Callout type="info" title="Values are shown in your units">

  Glucose values in the editor follow the unit you have chosen in your settings,
  mg/dL or mmol/L. Nocturne stores them in mg/dL and converts when it shows
  them, so a rule written in one unit reads correctly in the other.

</Callout>

## Channels

Channels are where the alert goes. A rule can have several, and they are all
sent at the same moment. A rule with no channels still fires and is still
recorded in alert history; it just does not notify anyone.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-channels" />

| Channel | Where the alert goes |
|---------|----------------------|
| **Browser Push** | A notification from your browser, even when no Nocturne tab is open. |
| **In-App** | The Nocturne notification centre and the alert banner in the app. |
| **Discord**, **Slack**, **Telegram**, **WhatsApp** | A direct message to a linked account, or a post in a channel or group the Nocturne bot has joined. See [Chat bots](/docs/bots). |
| **Email** | An email to an address you choose. Needs email set up first; see [Email alerts](/docs/alerts/email). |
| **Webhook** | A message sent to a web address you control, for connecting other systems. |
| **Device** | An instruction sent to a device you have registered with Nocturne. |

The channels on offer depend on what your site has set up. A chat channel only
appears once that chat app is connected.

## Auto-resolve

Auto-resolve closes an alert when a second condition becomes true, even if the
rule's own condition still holds. Most rules do not need it: an alert already
closes by itself as soon as its condition stops being true.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-auto-resolve" />

Auto-resolve is for "stop telling me once this has happened". For example: a
high alert that should stop once glucose has been coming down steadily for 15
minutes, even though it is still above the line. How it behaves, including what
happens if things get worse again, is on
[When an alert fires](/docs/alerts/when-an-alert-fires#auto-resolve).

**Suggest** fills in a starting condition by turning your rule's condition
around. Treat it as a draft. The exact opposite of your condition closes the
alert at the same moment it would have closed anyway.

## Smart snooze

Snoozing silences an alert for a while. Smart snooze keeps extending that
snooze, a few minutes at a time, while conditions you choose hold. The usual use
is "I have treated this low; stay quiet while glucose is climbing back".

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-smart-snooze" />

Details, including the limit on how many times a snooze can be extended, are on
[When an alert fires](/docs/alerts/when-an-alert-fires#snoozing).

## Testing a rule

The panel on the right has two ways to check a rule before you rely on it:

- **Fire saved rule** sends a real test alert to every channel on the saved
  rule, marked as a test. Use it to check the alert actually reaches the phone
  or chat it should.
- **Replay against history** runs the rule, including changes you have not
  saved yet, against your own past readings, and shows when it would have
  fired. Nothing is sent. See [Testing rules](/docs/alerts/testing).

Under it, **Historic firings** lists the times this rule has really fired.
Pick one to replay that day.

<Callout type="tip" title="Test with a real alert once">

  Replay proves the condition is right; only a real test alert proves the alert
  reaches you. After creating or changing an important rule, press **Fire saved
  rule** and check it arrives where you expect, including on a locked phone.

</Callout>
